  "question": {
    "text": "If the equations of the pairs of opposite sides of a parallelogram are \\(x^2-5 x+6=0\\) and \\(y^2-6 y+5=0\\), then equations of its diagonals are",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "C1ERA",
      "text": "\\(x+4 y=13, y=4 x-7\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "MRTJO",
      "text": "\\(4 x+y=13,4 y=x-7\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "BGX62",
      "text": "\\(4 x+y=13, y=4 x-7\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "YbedB",
      "text": "\\(y-4 x=13, y+4 x=7\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Equations of the sides of the parallelogram are\n\\((x-3)(x-2)=0\\) and \\((y-5)(y-1)=0\\)\ni.e. \\(x=3, x=2 ; y=5, y=1\\)\nHence its vertices are : \\(\\mathrm{A}(2,1) ; \\mathrm{B}(3,1)\\); \\(\\mathrm{C}(3,5) ; \\mathrm{D}(2,5)\\)\nEquation of the diagonal \\(\\mathrm{AC}\\) is\n\\(y-1=\\frac{4}{1}(x-2) \\Rightarrow y=4 x-7\\)\nEquation of the diagonal \\(\\mathrm{BD}\\) is\n\\(y-1=\\frac{4}{-1}(x-3) \\Rightarrow 4 x+y=13\\)",
    "image": null
  },
